1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,584
When you hear inflation

2
00:00:01,584 --> 00:00:03,168
is three percent, that

3
00:00:03,168 --> 00:00:04,752
number isn't tracking one

4
00:00:04,752 --> 00:00:05,940
price. It's tracking

5
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:06,336
thousands.

6
00:00:06,336 --> 00:00:07,861
The Bureau of Labor

7
00:00:07,861 --> 00:00:09,386
Statistics tracks a fixed

8
00:00:09,386 --> 00:00:10,912
basket of goods and

9
00:00:10,912 --> 00:00:12,437
services every single month.

10
00:00:12,437 --> 00:00:13,316
Housing, food,

11
00:00:13,316 --> 00:00:14,195
transportation, medical

12
00:00:14,195 --> 00:00:15,953
care — each category

13
00:00:15,953 --> 00:00:17,710
gets weighted by how

14
00:00:17,710 --> 00:00:19,468
much the average household

15
00:00:19,468 --> 00:00:21,226
actually spends on it.

16
00:00:21,226 --> 00:00:22,758
That's why the headline

17
00:00:22,758 --> 00:00:24,289
number can feel wrong.

18
00:00:24,289 --> 00:00:25,821
Your own spending mix

19
00:00:25,821 --> 00:00:27,352
almost never matches the

20
00:00:27,352 --> 00:00:28,501
national average basket.

21
00:00:28,501 --> 00:00:29,913
If you spend heavily

22
00:00:29,913 --> 00:00:31,325
on rent and gas,

23
00:00:31,325 --> 00:00:32,738
your real inflation can

24
00:00:32,738 --> 00:00:34,150
run well above or

25
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:35,562
below the reported rate.

26
00:00:35,562 --> 00:00:37,089
The CPI isn't wrong.

27
00:00:37,089 --> 00:00:38,616
It's just an average

28
00:00:38,616 --> 00:00:40,143
— and nobody actually

29
00:00:40,143 --> 00:00:41,670
lives an average life.

30
00:00:41,670 --> 00:00:42,815
Follow for more.
